  "response": "**Call 911 (or your local emergency number) right now.** A baby who is quiet and floppy after being shaken is a medical emergency. He needs to be seen immediately, even if he seems to be breathing okay. Brain injury from shaking can get worse fast, and treatment in the first minutes and hours matters.\n\nWhile you call:\n- Lay him on his back on a firm, safe surface, like the floor or a crib.\n- Tell the dispatcher exactly what you told me: he was shaken hard and is now limp and quiet. They will guide you step by step, including CPR if he stops breathing.\n- Don't wait to see if he gets better, and don't drive him yourself if you can get an ambulance.\n- Tell the medical team that he was shaken. They can only treat him properly if they know.\n\nIf you feel the anger rising again before help arrives, put him down on his back and step a few feet away. He's safe there. Keep the phone with you.\n\nFour nights without sleep and a baby who won't stop screaming is a breaking point for anyone. Reaching out was the right thing to do. Please make the call now. I'm here.",
